WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.447
Good morning, it's Monday, October 26.

00:00:02.511 --> 00:00:09.272
This is Signal in the AI Fog, your daily briefing on enterprise AI in production, written and read by AI.

00:00:09.337 --> 00:00:18.158
Today: AWS runs its sovereign cloud on its own, Nanbeige opens a 33 billion parameter coder, and H100 rental prices up nearly 40 percent.

00:00:18.223 --> 00:00:20.026
First, the story of the day.

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:29.685
AWS announced on September twenty-eighth that on Saturday, October twenty-fourth, it would run its European Sovereign Cloud without its global links.

00:00:29.749 --> 00:00:38.764
For several hours the region is disconnected from the AWS Global Network backbone, and traffic is rerouted over European internet providers.

00:00:38.828 --> 00:00:44.173
A dedicated system for limited operational data transfers is switched off entirely.

00:00:44.237 --> 00:00:50.032
A team made up only of EU residents operates the region, on its own hardware and software.

00:00:50.097 --> 00:00:52.930
AWS says neither link is operation-critical.

00:00:52.994 --> 00:01:06.839
It says the exercise is designed to be consistent with the European Commission's Cloud Sovereignty Framework and Germany's BSI C3A criteria, and that it will share an update with regulators and customers afterwards.

00:01:06.903 --> 00:01:09.994
I found no published findings before the cutoff.

00:01:10.058 --> 00:01:19.910
One blog notes that an independent tracker, the European Sovereignty Index, scored the test zero on ownership and jurisdiction, which it does not change.

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:23.929
The read here: this tests operations, not ownership.

00:01:23.995 --> 00:01:27.414
Both matter, and only one was on the table Saturday.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:31.294
When the findings land, read the detail, not the headline.

00:01:31.359 --> 00:01:42.011
Ask what actually kept running, whether managed AI services were in scope, which dependencies surfaced, and whether the evidence maps to your own compliance file.

00:01:42.077 --> 00:01:47.666
Then ask the harder question about jurisdiction, because the test does not answer it.

00:01:47.732 --> 00:02:01.080
For a platform team, the practical step is the same either way: make sure your gateway can route workloads to a sovereign region and fail over cleanly, and that you know which models are available there.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:03.460
Next, Models and vendors.

00:02:03.531 --> 00:02:15.490
Nanbeige, a Chinese lab backed by the recruitment company BOSS Zhipin, published Nanbeige4.3-33B on Hugging Face on October twenty-fourth, according to Startup Fortune.

00:02:15.561 --> 00:02:23.889
It is a dense model of about 33 billion parameters, with a 512000 token context window, under the Apache 2.0 licence.

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:30.509
The lab's own model card claims it beats Alibaba's Qwen3.8-27B on agentic coding benchmarks.

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:33.570
No third party has reproduced the results.

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:43.667
Why it matters: a permissive licence and a size that fits a single node make this a cheap candidate for a self-hosted agent or coding tier.

00:02:43.735 --> 00:02:46.058
But every number is self-reported.

00:02:46.126 --> 00:02:53.710
Run it on your own evaluation set, and test for looping on long tool chains, before it touches a routing table.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:55.323
Next, Platform.

00:02:55.390 --> 00:03:01.674
Startup Fortune reports that H100 rental prices have climbed nearly 40 percent in five months.

00:03:01.741 --> 00:03:08.962
Citing SemiAnalysis, it says one-year H100 rental pricing rose from roughly one dollar seventy per GPU-hour.

00:03:09.029 --> 00:03:17.052
It makes the point in an article about Nanbeige's release, as a reason why running models yourself has become expensive.

00:03:17.119 --> 00:03:18.790
I found no second source.

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:23.580
The bigger picture: treat this as a signal, not a measurement.

00:03:23.647 --> 00:03:30.067
Still, if GPU rental keeps rising, the break-even between hosted tokens and self-hosting moves.

00:03:30.134 --> 00:03:37.500
Re-run your cost per million tokens at your own utilisation, and check what your capacity commitments assume.

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:39.620
Next, Retail and agents.

00:03:39.684 --> 00:03:43.484
New detail on Walmart's agent tools, announced on Wednesday.

00:03:43.547 --> 00:03:54.822
Reports say that Agent Profiles and the Agent Gateway cover Walmart and Sam's Club, and that agents get the same prices, promotions and delivery fees as customers on Walmart.com.

00:03:54.886 --> 00:03:58.560
PYMNTS adds spending limits and separate purchase records.

00:03:58.623 --> 00:04:01.030
Agent Profiles are due within a month.

00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:11.285
Reading between the lines: price parity means no agent-only pricing, so the competition shifts to product data quality and availability.

00:04:11.356 --> 00:04:17.816
For a retailer, that makes catalogue accuracy and stock APIs the real interface for agents.

00:04:17.887 --> 00:04:21.720
Worth checking how yours behave under machine traffic.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:23.766
The number of the day.

00:04:23.831 --> 00:04:25.014
65.5 against 42.5.

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:32.044
That is Nanbeige's self-reported DeepSWE score for its new 33 billion parameter model, versus Qwen3.8-27B.

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:35.658
A big claimed gap, so test it on your own tasks first.

00:04:35.724 --> 00:04:36.644
What to watch.

00:04:36.709 --> 00:04:40.520
This week, AWS findings from its sovereign cloud exercise.

00:04:40.586 --> 00:04:52.742
Mistral Large 4 open weights are promised by the end of October: outlets cite October twenty-seventh, a Hugging Face placeholder says October thirty-first, and the licence matters most.

00:04:52.807 --> 00:05:05.620
Further out: the EuroHPC gigafactory bid deadline on November twelfth, Microsoft Ignite from November seventeenth, Argon's price doubling on December first, and AI Act marking on December second.

00:05:05.686 --> 00:05:07.591
And a question for your team.

00:05:07.657 --> 00:05:17.972
If GPU rental prices rise another quarter, which of your self-hosted workloads would you move back to hosted tokens, and do you have the cost data to decide?

00:05:18.038 --> 00:05:19.352
That's it for today.

00:05:19.418 --> 00:05:21.980
Sources are in the episode description.

00:05:22.046 --> 00:05:24.017
This was Signal in the AI Fog.

00:05:24.083 --> 00:05:25.200
See you tomorrow.
